{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For a half-life calculation: divide elapsed time by half-life to find n (number of half-lives)",
   "Remaining activity = initial activity × (1/2)ⁿ",
   "For alpha decay: mass number − 4, atomic number − 2; alpha particle is ⁴₂He",
   "For beta decay: mass number unchanged, atomic number + 1; beta particle is ⁰₋₁e"
  ],
  "title": "Half-Life and Nuclear Equations",
  "content": "<p>Two distinct skills tested together.</p><p><strong>Half-life calculations:</strong> Divide the total time by the half-life to get n. Then multiply the starting activity by (1/2) n times. Each halving step must be shown.</p><p><strong>Nuclear equations:</strong> Must balance on both sides — check mass numbers and atomic numbers separately. Alpha: lose 4 from mass, lose 2 from atomic number. Beta: mass unchanged, atomic number increases by 1.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Check both mass numbers AND atomic numbers balance — one side of the equation alone is not enough</li><li>Don’t try to shortcut half-life by dividing activity by n — use repeated halving</li></ul>"
 },
